===Glyph origin===
{{Han etym}}
{{liushu|p}} – originally a carriage seen from above. In the oracle bone script, there were large wheels on both sides and a sun shade on the top. Later, when Chinese characters were written vertically, the wheels on both sides were simply drawn in strokes and the loading area was marked with a {{och-l|田}}. Therefore, it is important to understand that the current character, {{och-l|車}} is a vertical depiction of a carriage.

Note that {{och-l|倝}} and {{och-l|朝}} are not derived from {{och-l|車}}.

===Etymology===
{{etymon|zh|id=vehicle|bor|unc|ine-toc-pro>*kuk(ä)le>wagon|tree=0}}
Perhaps a loan from an {{der|zh|ine}} language because the horse and chariot were introduced into China around 1200 BC from Inner Asia; compare {{cog|xto|kukäl}}, {{cog|txb|kokale||wagon; cart}} ({{zh-ref|Mair, 1990}}, {{zh-ref|Bauer, 1994}}), from {{der|zh|ine-toc-pro|*kuk(ä)le}}, from {{der|zh|ine-pro|*kʷékʷlos}}, a form of {{m|ine-pro|*kʷel-||to turn}}. Cognate with {{cog|en|wheel}}, {{cog|grc|κύκλος}}, {{cog|lt|kaklas||neck}}, etc.

[https://www.academia.edu/124180712/%E8%BB%8A_presented Baxter and Sagart] specifically pinpoint descent from some reflex of {{bor|zh|iir-pro|*čakrám}}. They also suppose that the variation of pronunciations could be attributed to separate borrowing events from Indo-Aryan and Iranian.

An older variant survives in Mandarin {{zh-l|軲轆|wheel}} ({{zh-ref|Bauer, 1994}}). Alternatively, the word is a derivation by k-prefix from {{och-l|舁|to lift}} ({{zh-ref|Baxter and Sagart, 1998}}); compare the semantic parallel in {{cog|bo|ཐེག་པ||vehicle; carriage}} (<to support; to carry; to lift).

Pronunciations 1 and 2 are cognate. A similar phonological doublet is {{och-l|處}} and {{och-l|居|id=2}} ({{zh-ref|Schuessler, 2007}}). Pronunciation 2 is traditionally regarded as the older pronunciation.

The sense of lewd content is derived from {{m|zh|老司機}}.

